    "finding": "The technology is bounded here as LLM-based agentic AI: language-model systems that can obtain external information or invoke tools and iteratively perform bounded multi-step tasks. It is not synonymous with AI generally, generative AI generally, a particular vendor product, or fully autonomous AI. The supplied primary evidence supports an evolutionary/recombination account rather than a single-invention account. In 2017, Vaswani et al. introduced the Transformer, an attention-based architecture that removed recurrence and convolution from the core sequence-transduction architecture and demonstrated greater parallelizability in the reported translation experiments. This supplied an important architectural predecessor, but did not itself establish LLM agents or enterprise task performance. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762?utm_source=chatgpt.com) In 2020, Brown et al. showed that scaling an autoregressive language model enabled task performance from instructions and examples without task-specific gradient updates, while also documenting tasks on which GPT-3 struggled. This contributed flexible language-mediated task adaptation, not dependable domain judgment. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165?utm_source=chatgpt.com) Also in 2020, Lewis et al. combined pretrained parametric generation with explicit non-parametric retrieval and reported improvements on knowledge-intensive NLP tasks. This supplied an important mechanism for grounding generation in externally retrieved material and for updating accessible knowledge without relying solely on model parameters; the paper itself identifies knowledge access, provenance and updating as problems motivating the approach. Retrieval, however, does not establish that a retrieved source is current, authoritative or applicable. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401?utm_source=chatgpt.com) In 2022, Ouyang et al. demonstrated supervised fine-tuning plus reinforcement learning from human feedback to improve instruction following according to human evaluations, while explicitly reporting that the resulting models still made simple mistakes. This contributes greater controllability through natural-language instructions but not factual correctness or organizational authority. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155?utm_source=chatgpt.com) Yao et al.'s ReAct work, first posted in October 2022 and published at ICLR 2023, interleaved language-model reasoning traces with task-specific actions, allowing models to update plans and obtain information from external environments; its evidence came from specified QA, fact-verification and interactive benchmarks rather than enterprise procurement. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629?utm_source=chatgpt.com) Schick et al.'s 2023 Toolformer supplied further evidence that language models can learn when and how to invoke external APIs and incorporate returned results, including search, calculation and other tools. Tool invocation capability does not establish permission to access organizational systems, correct tool choice in an unseen enterprise workflow, or safe decision authority. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761?utm_source=chatgpt.com) Taken together, the evidence supports a lineage of transformer-based language processing -> scaled in-context/few-shot task adaptation -> retrieval of external knowledge -> improved instruction following -> interleaving language reasoning with external actions -> learned API/tool invocation. Agentic AI is therefore best understood here as a recombination and extension of predecessor capabilities rather than a discrete isolated invention. The evidence packet supports technical opportunities/limitations motivating individual transitions—for example, limited parametric knowledge access motivating retrieval and separation of reasoning from acting motivating ReAct—but does not establish that today's hypothetical procurement problem historically caused the technology's emergence. The curren